      "@type": "Question",
      "name": "How much does drain field replacement cost?",
      "acceptedAnswer": {
        "@type": "Answer",
        "text": "Drain field replacement in Miami-Dade typically costs $5,000-$15,000 depending on size, soil conditions, and installation complexity. Small drain fields cost $5,000-$8,000. Standard drain fields cost $8,000-$15,000. Large drain fields cost $12,000-$20,000. Costs include removal, excavation, installation, permits, and inspection."
      }
    },
    {
      "@type": "Question",
      "name": "When do you need to replace a drain field?",
      "acceptedAnswer": {
        "@type": "Answer",
        "text": "Replacement is needed when drain fields fail completely, become permanently saturated, have severe damage beyond repair, show repeated repair failures, or when repair costs exceed 50% of replacement costs. Very old drain fields (20-30 years) may need replacement. Professional inspection determines whether replacement or repair is appropriate."
      }
    },
    {
      "@type": "Question",
      "name": "How long does drain field replacement take?",
      "acceptedAnswer": {
        "@type": "Answer",
        "text": "Replacement timeline typically takes 2-4 weeks from permit approval through final inspection. Permit approval takes 4-8 weeks before work begins. Physical replacement takes 8-12 days. Final inspection takes 3-5 days. Total timeline from permit application to completion typically takes 6-12 weeks depending on permit approval time and site complexity."
      }
    },
    {
      "@type": "Question",
      "name": "Can you repair a drain field instead of replacing?",
      "acceptedAnswer": {
        "@type": "Answer",
        "text": "Minor repairs are often sufficient for localized problems or when repair costs are less than 50% of replacement. However, replacement is needed for complete failure, permanent saturation, severe damage, repeated repair failures, or when repair costs exceed replacement value. Professional evaluation determines appropriate option."
      }
    },
    {
      "@type": "Question",
      "name": "What are signs you need drain field replacement?",
      "acceptedAnswer": {
        "@type": "Answer",
        "text": "Signs include persistent standing water over drain field, sewage backing up into home, foul odors continuously, slow drains throughout house that persist, soil saturation preventing drainage, complete loss of drainage function, and professional inspection findings indicating replacement needed. Professional inspection provides definitive assessment."
      }
    },
    {
      "@type": "Question",
      "name": "Do you need permits to replace a drain field?",
      "acceptedAnswer": {
        "@type": "Answer",
        "text": "Yes, complete drain field replacement requires prior written approval from Miami-Dade DERM Director before replacement begins. Required documentation includes replacement plans, site evaluation results, soil testing data, and permit application forms. New drain fields must meet Type 2, Type 3, or Type 4 standards. Professional contractors handle permit applications."
      }
    },
    {
      "@type": "Question",
      "name": "What is included in drain field replacement?",
      "acceptedAnswer": {
        "@type": "Answer",
        "text": "Replacement includes removal of old drain field, site evaluation and soil testing, drain field design meeting standards, excavation, new drain field installation, final inspection and approval, and system registration with DERM. Professional contractors provide comprehensive replacement services ensuring compliance with Miami-Dade requirements."
      }
    }
  ]
}

