      "name": "How much does grease trap installation cost for restaurants?",
      "acceptedAnswer": {
        "@type": "Answer",
        "text": "Grease trap installation for restaurants in Miami-Dade typically costs $2,000-$15,000 depending on trap size, material type, installation location, and complexity. Small traps cost $1,500-$3,000. Medium traps cost $3,000-$8,000. Large traps cost $8,000-$15,000. Costs include trap, plumbing connections, installation, and permits."
      }
    },
    {
      "@type": "Question",
      "name": "What is a grease trap and why do restaurants need them?",
      "acceptedAnswer": {
        "@type": "Answer",
        "text": "Grease traps intercept fats, oils, greases (FOG), and solid food waste before entering municipal wastewater system, separating materials by density to prevent pipe blockages. Restaurants produce substantially more FOG than residential kitchens. Most local governments including Miami-Dade legally require restaurants to maintain grease traps to protect municipal sewer systems and prevent environmental pollution."
      }
    },
    {
      "@type": "Question",
      "name": "What are Miami-Dade requirements for grease traps?",
      "acceptedAnswer": {
        "@type": "Answer",
        "text": "Miami-Dade County requires permits for installation, professional installation by licensed contractors, compliance with commercial wastewater regulations, health department inspection and approval, proper trap sizing, regular maintenance documentation, and trade waste agreement may be required. Grease traps legally required for restaurants with compliance mandatory for business license approval."
      }
    },
    {
      "@type": "Question",
      "name": "How often do grease traps need cleaning?",
      "acceptedAnswer": {
        "@type": "Answer",
        "text": "Grease traps typically need cleaning every 30-90 days depending on restaurant volume, usage patterns, and grease accumulation rate. High-volume restaurants may need monthly cleaning. Regular inspection determines appropriate cleaning schedule. Professional cleaning services remove accumulated grease and document cleaning for compliance records."
      }
    },
    {
      "@type": "Question",
      "name": "What size grease trap do I need for my restaurant?",
      "acceptedAnswer": {
        "@type": "Answer",
        "text": "Trap size depends on kitchen flow rate, peak service times, number of sinks, restaurant capacity, and type of cooking operations. Small restaurants typically need 100-500 gallons. Medium restaurants need 500-1,000 gallons. Large restaurants need 1,000-2,000 gallons. Professional contractors calculate appropriate size based on specific needs and local code requirements."
      }
    },
    {
      "@type": "Question",
      "name": "Do restaurants need permits for grease trap installation?",
      "acceptedAnswer": {
        "@type": "Answer",
        "text": "Yes, grease trap installation requires Miami-Dade County permit before installation begins. Required documentation includes trap specifications, installation plans, plumbing schematics, and compliance with plumbing codes. Health department review and approval also required. Permits ensure compliance with county regulations and health department codes."
      }
    },
    {
      "@type": "Question",
      "name": "What happens if I don't have a grease trap?",
      "acceptedAnswer": {
        "@type": "Answer",
        "text": "Without grease trap, restaurants face risks including pipe blockages, sewer backups, environmental pollution, health department violations and fines, business license revocation, and potential legal liability. Grease traps legally required for restaurants in Miami-Dade County. Compliance mandatory for restaurant operation and business license approval."
      }
    }
  ]
