    {
      "@type": "Question",
      "name": "How much does septic system installation cost?",
      "acceptedAnswer": {
        "@type": "Answer",
        "text": "New septic system installation in Miami-Dade typically costs $8,000-$25,000 depending on system type, size, site conditions, and installation complexity. Type 2 systems cost $8,000-$15,000. Type 3 systems cost $12,000-$20,000. Type 4 systems cost $15,000-$30,000. Professional contractors provide detailed estimates based on specific property needs."
      }
    },
    {
      "@type": "Question",
      "name": "How long does it take to install a septic system?",
      "acceptedAnswer": {
        "@type": "Answer",
        "text": "Installation timeline typically takes 2-6 months from initial site evaluation through final approval. Site evaluation takes 1-2 weeks, system design takes 2-4 weeks, permit approval takes 4-12 weeks, installation takes 1-2 weeks, and inspection takes 1-2 weeks. Timeline varies based on application completeness and site complexity."
      }
    },
    {
      "@type": "Question",
      "name": "What permits do you need to install a septic system?",
      "acceptedAnswer": {
        "@type": "Answer",
        "text": "New installations require prior written approval from Miami-Dade DERM Director, site plan, building plan, site evaluation results, system design specifications, and permit application forms. Permit fees cost $1,800-$5,800 typically. Professional contractors handle permit applications and coordinate with DERM."
      }
    },
    {
      "@type": "Question",
      "name": "What type of septic system is required in Miami?",
      "acceptedAnswer": {
        "@type": "Answer",
        "text": "Miami-Dade County requires Type 2, Type 3, or Type 4 systems for new installations. Type 1 conventional systems are prohibited effective January 1, 2023. Type 2 is for single-family homes. Type 3 is for multifamily properties. Type 4 is required when Type 2 or Type 3 criteria aren't met. System type depends on property usage and site conditions."
      }
    },
    {
      "@type": "Question",
      "name": "How do you install a new septic system?",
      "acceptedAnswer": {
        "@type": "Answer",
        "text": "Installation includes site evaluation and soil testing, system design meeting requirements, permit application to DERM, permit approval, professional installation by licensed contractor, final inspection, and system registration. Professional contractors manage entire process ensuring compliance and proper installation."
      }
    },
    {
      "@type": "Question",
      "name": "What is the process for installing a septic system?",
      "acceptedAnswer": {
        "@type": "Answer",
        "text": "Process includes site evaluation, system design, permit application, permit approval, professional installation, final inspection, and system registration. Each step requires professional expertise and compliance with regulations. Professional contractors coordinate all phases to complete installations efficiently."
      }
    },
    {
      "@type": "Question",
      "name": "Can I install my own septic system?",
      "acceptedAnswer": {
        "@type": "Answer",
        "text": "Owner-occupied single-family homes may have exemptions, but all installations require permits, inspections, and compliance. Professional installation is strongly recommended because systems must meet strict requirements, proper installation ensures function, mistakes are expensive to fix, and contractors understand regulations ensuring compliance."
      }
    }
  ]
}

